    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The formation of fractures with large aperture to length ratios is discussed for cemented fractures in septarian concretions and chert spheroids, and for uncemented fractures in combustion metamorphosed siliceous shale. In all three instances, opening of large-aperture fractures is accompanied by significant volume reduction of the surrounding matrix, apparently due to mass transport by dissolution-precipitation reactions. It is inferred that matrix contraction and resulting formation of large-aperture fractures is due to effective tensile stresses induced by gradients in chemical potential driving dissolution of matrix minerals between contacting grains. This process may be fairly indiscriminate of the mineral system involved, potentially capable of producing large contractile strain in a variety of diagenetic and structural settings. The inferred process may cause isotropic tensile stress states in two or three principal directions leading to polygonal or orthorhombic fracture systems and mosaic breccias. This process may also cause stress gradients leading, for instance, to joint clusters that are difficult to explain by mechanical loading alone.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
